      "type": "text",
      "text": "{\"unit_id\": \"Harweben\", \"choices\": [], \"reasoning\": \"Record 1, Harweben, is Har-weben, 'Horus shines' (Ḥr-wbn): the god Horus followed by the verb wbn, 'to rise, shine'. C1, Hr-jb-Nj.t, is Her-ib-Neith, made of the preposition ḥr, the noun jb 'heart' and the goddess Neith. On consonants alone a forced reading is possible: the weak j may be rendered w, and the feminine ending .t of Nj.t may be omitted. Read as a modern spelling, though, Harweben renders different words. Har stands for Horus, not the preposition 'Her', and 'weben' is the verb wbn, not jb plus Neith. Neith would also not be written as a bare 'n' with its ending dropped. C1 is a different name.\"}"
